==================================================
Lab 1: OS Command Injection (Simple Case)
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website has a stock checker that tells you if a product is available at a chosen store.
To check this, the website runs a shell command on its server, using the product ID and store ID you provide.
It does not clean this input, so we can inject our own shell commands into it.

Goal: run the whoami command and get back the name of the current server user.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When you check stock, the browser sends a request with a productId and a storeId.
The server likely runs something like a shell script that checks stock using these two values together,
for example checking stock for storeId 1. The output of that command is then shown back to you on the page.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The storeId value is placed directly inside a shell command on the server, with no filtering.
Since shells allow more than one command to run in sequence, whatever we add after a semicolon
gets executed as a separate, second command.

===========================
ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Semicolon: In a shell, a semicolon separates two commands written on the same line.
The first command runs, then the second one runs right after it, regardless of what the first one did.
So storeId equal to 1; whoami means run the stock check for store 1, then run whoami.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of sending a normal storeId like 1, we send 1; whoami.

The server builds a command using this value, so the final command becomes something like:

check_stock 1; whoami

The shell treats this as two separate commands. It checks stock for store 1, then runs whoami right after.
The output of whoami, which is the name of the current server user, gets printed along with the normal response,
and the raw output is shown back to us in the page.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and go to any product page.
Intercept the stock check request, or use the browser dev tools to see the request being sent.
Change the storeId value from 1 to 1; whoami.
Send the request. The response now includes the username, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It imports requests and starts a session, which keeps cookies between requests, similar to a browser.
* It asks for the lab's base URL, then builds the full target URL by adding /product/stock to it.
* It builds the payload. productId is left as a normal value, 1, while storeId is set to 1; whoami,
  so the semicolon injects our command after the real one.
* It sends a POST request to the stock check endpoint with this payload.
* If the server responds with status 200, it prints the raw response text, which should include the
  output of whoami along with the normal stock message.
* If the connection fails or the server returns a different status, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The server takes user input and places it directly inside a shell command without separating the two.
Because the shell reads semicolons as command separators, anything we add after one gets executed
as its own command, with the same permissions as the application.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Avoid passing user input directly into shell commands.
If a system command must be used, pass user input as a properly escaped argument, never concatenated
into the command string, or use safer APIs that do not invoke a shell at all.

Other good practices:

Validate input strictly, allowing only expected characters, like digits for an ID.
Run application processes with the lowest possible permissions.
Never return raw command output to the user.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Never trust anything typed by a user.
A semicolon in a shell command separates two commands, letting a second one run right after the first.
Returning raw command output to the page gives an attacker direct feedback, making this kind of bug easy to exploit.
